findings.body:
`Skeleton` is imported on line 6 but never used. The `<Suspense>` on line 12 has no `fallback`, so the import is dead. Unused imports are usually flagged by the repo's linter and typecheck setup. I did not run the linter to confirm this for this repo.

The import is probably left over from copying `FlowLink`. Either remove it or, more likely, use it as the fallback (see the next finding).

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-icon-text/flow-icon-text.tsx" lines="1-12"}

The PR description says the component "follows the established Suspense pattern ... with Skeleton fallback". `FlowLink` and `DeploymentLink` both pass `fallback={<Skeleton className="h-4 w-full" />}`. Here the boundary is a bare `<Suspense>`, so while the flow query is pending nothing renders at all.

This matters because callers place `FlowIconText` in table cells and lists. Content will appear with a layout shift instead of holding a placeholder slot. The component also wraps itself in a `Suspense` rather than letting the caller decide, so a fallback is the only loading UI it has.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-icon-text/flow-icon-text.tsx" lines="10-16"}
export const FlowIconText = ({ flowId }: FlowIconTextProps) => {
	return (
		<Suspense fallback={<Skeleton className="h-4 w-full" />}>
			<FlowIconTextImplementation flowId={flowId} />
		</Suspense>
	);
};

In the story decorator, `createTestRouter` passes `context: { queryClient: new QueryClient() }` to the router. `FlowIconText` never reads router context. It reads the query client from the `QueryClientProvider` that the decorator renders around `RouterProvider`, which is the module-level `queryClient`. The router's client is a separate instance and is never used.

The unused client is misleading to someone copying the story. It is also created on every decorator render. Drop the `context` option, or make it reuse the provider's client.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-icon-text/flow-icon-text.stories.tsx" lines="20-34"}

`FlowIconText` and `FlowLink` differ only by the "Flow" label text and the wrapper `div`. The query, link target, icon, and Suspense wiring are copied. Two components that must stay in sync for a route or query change are easy to let drift.

The author chose a separate component to avoid a label prop, and the description explains the intent, so this is a trade-off rather than a bug. If the team expects more variants, a `showLabel` option on `FlowLink` (or a shared inner component) would keep the logic in one place.

